                  <div class="top"><span class="tag rec">Recommended</span><h4>Full sweep</h4></div>
                  <p>
                    Correct these three, then the rest, so your name, address, phone and hours match
                    everywhere. Consistency across listings is one of the things Google uses to rank
                    local businesses.
                  </p>
                </div>
                <div class="opt alt">
                  <div class="top"><span class="tag alt">Smaller start</span><h4>The main three</h4></div>
                  <p>Just the listings appearing in searches and AI answers today.</p>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>
          </article>

          <article class="item" id="finding-06">
            <div class="item-head">
              <p class="n" aria-hidden="true">06</p>
              <div>
                <span class="badge">Worth doing</span>
                <h3>Your 4.9 rating isn&rsquo;t on your own site</h3>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="b">
              <p>
                There are member quotes on the homepage, but no rating, no review count, and no way for
                a member to leave one. It&rsquo;s your strongest proof, missing at the exact moment someone
                decides whether to book.
              </p>
              <div class="opts two">
                <div class="opt rec">
                  <div class="top"><span class="tag rec">Recommended</span><h4>Show it as text</h4></div>
                  <p>
                    Display the rating and count, link to your Google reviews, refresh occasionally. No
                    cost, nothing to break, and the number moves slowly enough to stay accurate.
                  </p>
                </div>
                <div class="opt alt">
                  <div class="top"><span class="tag alt">More moving parts</span><h4>Pull it automatically</h4></div>
                  <p>
                    Connect to Google so it updates itself. Small ongoing cost, limits on how many
                    reviews can be pulled, and rules on display. A lot of machinery for a number that
                    barely changes.
                  </p>
                </div>
              </div>
              <div class="notes">
                <p class="k">What this can and can&rsquo;t do</p>
                <p class="note can">
                  <strong>You can</strong> show your rating, your review count and your reviews on your
                  own website, and link people to your Google listing to read more or leave one.
                </p>
                <p class="note cannot">
                  <strong>You can&rsquo;t</strong> make star ratings appear under your listing in Google search results this way. <a href="https://developers.google.com/search/docs/appearance/structured-data/review-snippet" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Google&rsquo;s own documentation</a> says a business that controls the reviews about itself is &ldquo;ineligible for star review feature&rdquo;, and separately, &ldquo;don&rsquo;t aggregate reviews or ratings from other websites&rdquo;. If anyone offers you stars in search results from this, they&rsquo;re overselling.
                </p>
                <p class="note still">
                  <strong>Still worth doing,</strong> because it&rsquo;s the proof people look for in the
                  seconds before they book, and right now it isn&rsquo;t there.
                </p>
              </div>
            </div>
